# the argument of aquinas-st-i-2.md as claude-opus-5-5 read it: check that it says what the text says
# Aquinas, ST I q.42 a.1 ad 2, on the equality and likeness of the Son to the Father
atom	common_form	the same nature (common form) is in both Father and Son
atom	more_perfect	one of them participates in the form more perfectly than the other
atom	alike	the Son is like to the Father
atom	equal	the Son is equal to the Father
P1	S2	equal → alike	kind conceptual
P2	S3	common_form → alike	kind conceptual
P3	S3	more_perfect → ¬equal	kind conceptual
P4	S4	common_form ∧ ¬more_perfect
# the text treats a common form held in perfect equality as sufficient for equality
A1	-	common_form ∧ ¬more_perfect → equal
C	S4	alike ∧ equal	from P2 P4 A1
